var e=[{kind:`lesson`,id:`S1`,track:`sound`,title:`Hear`,status:`built, not flashed`,heading:`Hear what the microphones really send`,goal:`<span class="snd-parts"><span class="need">KY-037 or INMP441</span></span>Two microphones, two ways in. The <b>INMP441</b> is digital: its own amplifier and 24-bit converter, numbers over I2S. The KY-037 has two outputs. <b>AO</b> is the microphone's own voltage: a tiny wave riding on a steady <i>bias</i>. <b>DO</b> is a yes/no from the LM393 comparator, set by the blue trim pot. S1 samples AO 16 000 times a second with the ADC's DMA (no <code>analogRead()</code> in a loop), draws it as an oscilloscope, shows where it sits in the ADC's range, and lets you tune the pot. The <b>mics</b> view puts both side by side. Every other sound lab stands on this: the sampler, the 4-second ring and the level meter live in <code>kit/lib/sound-kit</code>.`,goalLine:`See your voice and a clap as a wave, compare the two microphones, read the bias (or the bits), and tune the comparator so it fires only with loud sounds.`,goalCheck:[`The <b>scope</b> shows a flat line while you're quiet and a wave when you talk; a whistle stands still (trigger on).`,`The <b>bias</b> view shows your module's bias in mV and no CLIPPING when you clap close to it; you tried <code>atten 6</code> and saw the step size halve.`,`On the <b>comparator</b> view, DO lights with claps and stays dark while you talk normally: “tuned”.`,`<code>status</code> says <code>16000 Hz measured</code> and <code>DMA overruns 0</code>.`,`With both mics wired, the <b>mics</b> view says how many dB more clearly the INMP441 hears a word.`],learn:[`analog vs digital mic`,`I2S`,`bias voltage`,`ADC attenuation`,`continuous ADC + DMA`,`ring buffer`,`6 dB per bit, headroom`,`comparator & hysteresis`,`dBFS`],pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`3V3`,idx:1,role:`MICS +`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`6`,role:`AO`,side:`L`,kind:`sound`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`5V`,idx:20,role:`AMP VIN`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`GND`,idx:0,role:`MICS G`,side:`R`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`42`,role:`SD`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-mic`},{pin:`41`,role:`WS`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-mic`},{pin:`40`,role:`SCK`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-mic`},{pin:`39`,role:`BCLK`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-amp`},{pin:`38`,role:`LRC`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-amp`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`},{pin:`47`,role:`DIN`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-amp`},{pin:`21`,role:`DO`,side:`R`,kind:`sound`},{pin:`GND`,idx:20,role:`AMP G`,side:`R`,kind:`gnd`}],boot:!0,cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/sound-lab-s1 -e sound-lab-s1 -t upload`,wiring:{title:`Wiring: the microphones and the speaker (wire what the lab needs; the screen stays as in lesson 03)`,head:[`Module pin`,`ESP32-S3`,`Notes`],rows:[[`KY-037 + · G`,`3V3`,`The analog mic. + on the second 3V3 pin (the screen has the first), G on GND. <b>Never 5 V</b>: AO and DO swing up to the module's supply, and the ESP32-S3's pins take 3.3 V at most.`],[`KY-037 AO`,`GPIO 6`,`Analog out: the microphone's voltage. GPIO 6 is ADC1 channel 5: ADC1 keeps working while Wi-Fi is on, ADC2 doesn't.`],[`KY-037 DO`,`GPIO 21`,`Digital out: the LM393 comparator says high when the sound is louder than the blue trim pot's setting.`],[`INMP441 VDD · GND`,`3V3`,`The digital mic (I2S). VDD on 3V3 (share the breadboard rail with the KY-037), GND on GND. 3.3 V only.`],[`INMP441 L/R`,`GND`,`Low = it talks in the left slot, the one the board reads.`],[`INMP441 SCK`,`GPIO 40`,`The bit clock. The ESP32 drives it: 16 000 × 64 = 1.024 MHz.`],[`INMP441 WS`,`GPIO 41`,`Word select: left slot / right slot, flips every 32 clock ticks.`],[`INMP441 SD`,`GPIO 42`,`The data, most significant bit first. Pulled down, so a missing mic reads as zeros.`],[`MAX98357A VIN · GND`,`5V`,`The speaker amplifier. 5V gives the most (about 3 W into 4 Ω); 3V3 works, quieter.`],[`MAX98357A BCLK`,`GPIO 39`,`Bit clock of the second I2S port (I2S1; the mic uses I2S0, so both run at once).`],[`MAX98357A LRC`,`GPIO 38`,`Word select (left/right).`],[`MAX98357A DIN`,`GPIO 47`,`The samples, 16-bit.`],[`MAX98357A GAIN · SD`,`nothing`,`Leave both unconnected: 9 dB gain, and it plays (left + right) / 2.`],[`speaker (4 Ω, 3 W)`,`amp + / −`,`On the amplifier's screw terminal: the JST-PH2.0 lead's two wires, red to +.`],[`screen (8 wires)`,`as lesson 03`,`Unchanged. The push button on GPIO 5 is optional: it does what BOOT does.`]]},commands:[{command:`uv run pio run -d labs/sound-lab-s1 -e sound-lab-s1 -t upload`,where:`mac`,card:`squiet`,does:`Builds lab S1 and writes it to the board, then open the monitor with <code>uv run pio device monitor</code>.`,see:`<code>Listening for an INMP441 on the I2S pins...</code>, <code>Microphone: INMP441 (I2S, digital) (mic setting: auto)</code> (or KY-037), then <code>Sound lab S1 …</code>, the command list and a three-note hello from the speaker. Stay quiet for half a second: <code>- learning the quiet...</code>`,fix:`Wrong mic picked: <code>mic inmp441</code> or <code>mic ky037</code> (saved, restarts). No hello: the speaker is optional; check VIN and DIN. “Failed to connect”: hold BOOT, tap RST, release BOOT, run it again.`},{command:`mic inmp441 (or mic ky037, mic auto)`,card:`sany`,does:`Which microphone the shell uses. <code>auto</code> (the default) listens for an INMP441 for 0.3 s at start-up. Saved in NVS (namespace <code>sound</code>); the board restarts.`,see:`<code>Microphone: INMP441 (I2S, digital) (mic setting: inmp441)</code>; top right of the screen a small <b>D</b> instead of the DO lamp.`,fix:`Stays on the KY-037: check SCK 40, WS 41, SD 42, L/R to GND.`},{command:`(click to the mics view, then talk)`,where:`button`,card:`ssound`,does:`Opens the other microphone too and shows both lanes, INMP441 on top: each one's quiet in dBFS and how far above it you are.`,see:`<code>INMP441 hears it 12 dB more clearly</code> (the number depends on your room); the KY-037's lane is grainier.`,fix:`Only one lane: the other mic isn't wired. Wire it, then press RST: S1 looks for it at start-up.`},{command:`thresh 20`,card:`ssound`,does:`With the INMP441 there is no DO pin: the board compares in software. thresh is the line in dB above the quiet (default 15), with 3 dB of hysteresis.`,see:`<code>- software comparator at 20 dB</code>; on the comparator view only claps cross it.`,fix:``},{command:`(talk, then clap, near the mic)`,where:`hands`,card:`ssound`,does:`The <b>scope</b> view draws the last 20 ms. Talking makes a small wobbly wave, a clap fills the box.`,see:`A wave in pink; the scale on the left grows to ±1-5 mV for a voice, ±100 mV and more for a clap.`,fix:`Flat line whatever you do: AO isn't on GPIO 6, or + isn't on 3V3 (the module's power LED must be on).`},{command:`status`,card:`sany`,does:`The sampler, the ADC and the level in numbers.`,see:`<code>sampler: 16000 Hz asked, 16000 Hz measured · frames dropped 0 · DMA overruns 0</code>, then <code>ADC: attenuation 12 dB (0-3.1 V) · bias … mV</code>.`,fix:`Overruns climbing: something blocks core 0 for long; tell Claude what else runs.`},{command:`(click to the bias view)`,where:`button`,card:`squiet`,does:`The 0-3.3 V ruler, your bias, and the swing of the last 0.1 s.`,see:`A bias line somewhere around 1-2 V and a hint such as <code>fits in 0-1.75 V: try atten 6 (2x finer)</code>.`,fix:`Bias near 0 or 3.3 V: the module is on 5 V or a wire is loose. Use 3V3.`},{command:`atten 6`,card:`sany`,does:`Narrows the ADC's range to 0-1.75 V: each step becomes 0.43 mV instead of 0.76. (Double-click on bias steps through 12 → 6 → 2.5 → 0.)`,see:`<code>- attenuation 6 dB (0-1.75 V)</code>; on the ruler the shaded range shrinks, the wave looks taller on the scope.`,fix:`<code>CLIPPING</code>: the bias or the wave no longer fits. Go back with <code>atten 12</code>.`},{command:`pot`,card:`squiet`,does:`Prints how to tune the comparator and jumps to the comparator view.`,see:`Three steps: quiet → turn until LED2 just goes off; clap → it flashes; adjust.`,fix:``},{command:`(turn the blue pot with a small screwdriver)`,where:`hands`,card:`ssound`,does:`Moves the comparator's threshold. Watch the DO lamp (top right) and the strip: loudness on top, DO below.`,see:`Tuned: <code>DO follows the loud moments: tuned</code>, LED2 flashes with claps, dark while you talk.`,fix:`<code>DO always on</code>: turn the other way. Which way is more sensitive differs between makers.`},{command:`plot`,card:`ssound`,does:`Prints the level as a text bar every 50 ms (any sound lab has it). <code>plot off</code> stops it.`,see:`<code> 23.4 dB |#########          |   | DO 0</code> lines scrolling.`,fix:``},{command:`dump 64`,card:`ssound`,does:`Prints the newest 64 samples as numbers (int16 after removing the bias; one ADC step = 16).`,see:`Four lines of 16 comma-separated numbers, e.g. <code>-32,16,48,…</code>. Paste into a spreadsheet and draw a line chart.`,fix:``},{command:`freeze, span 5`,card:`ssound`,does:`Freeze holds the picture (double-click on scope, or long press); span sets the time across the screen.`,see:`<code>scope frozen (freeze again to go on)</code>; with <code>span 5</code> single waves of a whistle become clear.`,fix:``}],panels:[{title:`The sound track, lab by lab`,widget:`soundJourney`,intro:`Six labs, each its own firmware, all reading the same microphone. Pick one to see what it turns sound into.`},{title:`From air to numbers`,widget:`soundChain`,intro:`Eight stages between your voice and a number the code can use. Step through them (or let it play); the buttons at the right make sounds, and every value below the picture is live.`},{title:`Analog mic, digital mic: how I2S carries a sample`,widget:`soundMics`,intro:`The KY-037 hands the ESP32 a voltage to measure; the INMP441 measures it itself and sends the number. Below: one I2S frame on its three wires. Step it tick by tick and watch a 24-bit sample arrive, then become the 16-bit number every lab uses.`},{title:`Where the wave sits in the ADC's range (KY-037)`,widget:`soundBias`,intro:`Move the bias and the loudness, change the attenuation. The ADC can only measure inside the shaded range: outside it the wave is cut flat (clipping). A smaller range gives finer steps, as long as the wave still fits.`},{title:`The comparator and its pot`,widget:`soundComparator`,intro:`The LM393 compares AO with the voltage the pot sets. Above the line, DO is high (and LED2 on the module lights). Drag the dashed line (or the slider) and watch DO: too low and it chatters all the time, too high and even a clap is missed.`},{title:`What the screen shows`,widget:`soundScreens`,intro:`Five views. Click = next view, double-click = the action at the bottom left, long press = freeze. Top right: the level bar, the DO lamp (or <b>D</b> with the INMP441), waves while the speaker plays, and a red dot whenever anything records (S4, S8).`}],steps:[`Wire the KY-037: + → 3V3 (the second pin), G → GND, AO → GPIO 6, DO → GPIO 21. The screen stays as in lesson 03. The module's power LED lights.`,`Flash <code>sound-lab-s1</code>, open the monitor, stay quiet for a second. <b>Checkpoint 1:</b> the <code>Mic: AO on GPIO 6 …</code> line and a flat line on the scope.`,`Talk, whistle and clap near the mic. <b>Checkpoint 2:</b> three different waves; the whistle stands still.`,`Click to <b>bias</b>, read your bias in mV, try the attenuation the hint suggests. <b>Checkpoint 3:</b> no CLIPPING when you clap 20 cm away.`,`Type <code>pot</code> and tune the comparator. <b>Checkpoint 4:</b> “tuned” on the comparator view.`,`Type <code>status</code>. <b>Checkpoint 5:</b> 16000 Hz measured, 0 overruns.`],note:`With the INMP441 the scope's scale is in % of full scale, the bias view becomes a ladder of its 24 bits (each bit = 6 dB; how many the sound uses, the headroom left), the comparator is software, and <b>numbers</b> adds dB SPL. Honest expectations for the KY-037: it has no amplifier. Its AO is the capsule's raw voltage, a few millivolts for a voice, so speech uses only a handful of the ADC's 4096 steps. That makes it a good <b>loudness</b> sensor (claps, knocks, noise, beats) and only a rough microphone. DO is a single comparator: a clap makes dozens of edges on it as the wave crosses the line, so the labs listen to AO and use DO for the hardware lamp and as a second opinion. The ADC samples through ADC1, which keeps working while Wi-Fi is on (ADC2 doesn't).`,preview:{widget:`sound`}},{kind:`lesson`,id:`S2`,track:`sound`,title:`Loudness`,status:`built, not flashed`,heading:`A sound level meter for your room`,goal:`<span class="snd-parts"><span class="need">KY-037 or INMP441</span><span class="opt">MAX98357A + speaker (optional)</span></span>How loud is it? With the <b>INMP441</b> S2 shows real <b>dB SPL</b> (its datasheet says −26 dBFS at 94 dB SPL, so RMS dBFS + 111): 30 whisper, 60 talk, 85 the hearing-risk line. With the KY-037 it shows dB above your room's quiet. S2 turns the samples into decibels the way a sound level meter does: <b>fast</b> (125 ms) or <b>slow</b> (1 s) smoothing, a peak hold, and a noise floor that learns your room. Then it keeps a minute of graph, a ten-minute noise diary, and an alarm that lights lesson 02's LED on GPIO 4 when it stays too loud.`,goalLine:`Read your room's level (in dB SPL with the INMP441), see the difference between fast and slow, keep a diary, and set off (and silence) the alarm.`,goalCheck:[`The <b>meter</b> reads about 0 when quiet, 15-25 when you talk, 40+ for a clap, and says what that roughly is.`,`With <code>speed slow</code> the number calms down; the peak tick still catches a clap.`,`After a few minutes, <code>diary</code> prints rows with low / avg / high.`,`Loud music for a second turns the <b>alarm</b> view red, lights the LED, and a button press silences it.`],learn:[`decibels (+6 = ×2)`,`RMS`,`dB SPL needs a known sensitivity`,`unweighted vs dBA`,`85 dB risk line`,`fast / slow smoothing`,`noise floor`,`echo guard`],pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`3V3`,idx:1,role:`MICS +`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`6`,role:`AO`,side:`L`,kind:`sound`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`5V`,idx:20,role:`AMP VIN`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`GND`,idx:0,role:`MICS G`,side:`R`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`42`,role:`SD`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-mic`},{pin:`41`,role:`WS`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-mic`},{pin:`40`,role:`SCK`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-mic`},{pin:`39`,role:`BCLK`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-amp`},{pin:`38`,role:`LRC`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-amp`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`},{pin:`47`,role:`DIN`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-amp`},{pin:`21`,role:`DO`,side:`R`,kind:`sound`},{pin:`GND`,idx:20,role:`AMP G`,side:`R`,kind:`gnd`}],boot:!0,cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/sound-lab-s2 -e sound-lab-s2 -t upload`,wiring:{title:`Wiring: the microphones and the speaker (wire what the lab needs; the screen stays as in lesson 03)`,head:[`Module pin`,`ESP32-S3`,`Notes`],rows:[[`KY-037 + · G`,`3V3`,`The analog mic. + on the second 3V3 pin (the screen has the first), G on GND. <b>Never 5 V</b>: AO and DO swing up to the module's supply, and the ESP32-S3's pins take 3.3 V at most.`],[`KY-037 AO`,`GPIO 6`,`Analog out: the microphone's voltage. GPIO 6 is ADC1 channel 5: ADC1 keeps working while Wi-Fi is on, ADC2 doesn't.`],[`KY-037 DO`,`GPIO 21`,`Digital out: the LM393 comparator says high when the sound is louder than the blue trim pot's setting.`],[`INMP441 VDD · GND`,`3V3`,`The digital mic (I2S). VDD on 3V3 (share the breadboard rail with the KY-037), GND on GND. 3.3 V only.`],[`INMP441 L/R`,`GND`,`Low = it talks in the left slot, the one the board reads.`],[`INMP441 SCK`,`GPIO 40`,`The bit clock. The ESP32 drives it: 16 000 × 64 = 1.024 MHz.`],[`INMP441 WS`,`GPIO 41`,`Word select: left slot / right slot, flips every 32 clock ticks.`],[`INMP441 SD`,`GPIO 42`,`The data, most significant bit first. Pulled down, so a missing mic reads as zeros.`],[`MAX98357A VIN · GND`,`5V`,`The speaker amplifier. 5V gives the most (about 3 W into 4 Ω); 3V3 works, quieter.`],[`MAX98357A BCLK`,`GPIO 39`,`Bit clock of the second I2S port (I2S1; the mic uses I2S0, so both run at once).`],[`MAX98357A LRC`,`GPIO 38`,`Word select (left/right).`],[`MAX98357A DIN`,`GPIO 47`,`The samples, 16-bit.`],[`MAX98357A GAIN · SD`,`nothing`,`Leave both unconnected: 9 dB gain, and it plays (left + right) / 2.`],[`speaker (4 Ω, 3 W)`,`amp + / −`,`On the amplifier's screw terminal: the JST-PH2.0 lead's two wires, red to +.`],[`screen (8 wires)`,`as lesson 03`,`Unchanged. The push button on GPIO 5 is optional: it does what BOOT does.`]]},commands:[{command:`uv run pio run -d labs/sound-lab-s2 -e sound-lab-s2 -t upload`,where:`mac`,card:`squiet`,does:`Builds lab S2 and writes it to the board, then open the monitor with <code>uv run pio device monitor</code>.`,see:`<code>Listening for an INMP441 on the I2S pins...</code>, <code>Microphone: INMP441 (I2S, digital) (mic setting: auto)</code> (or KY-037), then <code>Sound lab S2 …</code>, the command list and a three-note hello from the speaker. Stay quiet for half a second: <code>- learning the quiet...</code>`,fix:`Wrong mic picked: <code>mic inmp441</code> or <code>mic ky037</code> (saved, restarts). No hello: the speaker is optional; check VIN and DIN. “Failed to connect”: hold BOOT, tap RST, release BOOT, run it again.`},{command:`(talk, then clap)`,where:`hands`,card:`ssound`,does:`The <b>meter</b> view: the big number is dB above your room's quiet.`,see:`Around 15-25 while you talk (“talking”), 40+ on a clap (“shout, clap, bang”). The white tick is the peak hold.`,fix:`Always 0: the quiet was learnt while you were talking. Type <code>quiet</code> and stay silent for half a second.`},{command:`speed slow`,card:`ssound`,does:`Switches the meter, graphs and alarm to 1 s smoothing (double-click on meter toggles).`,see:`<code>- slow: 1 s smoothing</code>; the number moves calmly, a clap barely lifts it.`,fix:``},{command:`units quiet (units spl)`,card:`sany`,does:`Switches between dB SPL (INMP441 only) and dB above the quiet.`,see:`<code>- units: dB above the quiet</code>; <code>units spl</code> on the KY-037: <code>dB SPL needs the INMP441 (its sensitivity is known)</code>.`,fix:``},{command:`limit 75 spl (or limit 30 quiet)`,card:`sany`,does:`The alarm line: 30-120 dB SPL (default 80) or 3-60 dB above the quiet (default 35). Saved in NVS (namespace <code>loud</code>).`,see:`<code>- limit 75 dB SPL</code>; the dotted red line moves.`,fix:``},{command:`alarm sound off`,card:`sany`,does:`The alarm tune plays once per alarm on the speaker (the button stops it too). The echo guard keeps S2 from measuring its own tune.`,see:`<code>- alarm sound off</code>.`,fix:``},{command:`(play loud music for 2 s)`,where:`hands`,card:`smusic`,does:`Over the limit for <code>hold</code> (1 s) fires the alarm.`,see:`<code>x too loud: 38 dB</code>; the <b>alarm</b> view pulses red, the LED on GPIO 4 lights (if wired).`,fix:`No alarm: <code>alarm on</code>, or a lower <code>limit</code>.`},{command:`(press the button)`,where:`button`,card:`sany`,does:`While the alarm rings, any press silences it; it re-arms when the room is calm (3 dB under the line).`,see:`<code>- alarm silenced</code>, later <code>+ calm again</code>.`,fix:``},{command:`diary`,card:`sany`,does:`Prints the last ten minutes, one row per 10 s: quietest, average, loudest, and ALARM.`,see:`<code>  60s     2   11   34  |=====      |</code> rows.`,fix:`Empty: the diary fills one row every 10 s. Come back in a few minutes.`}],panels:[{title:`What a decibel is`,widget:`soundDecibels`,intro:`Our ears hear ratios, not differences, so loudness is measured in decibels: 20 × log10(this ÷ the quiet). Slide the loudness and watch the wave: every +6 dB doubles its height, +20 dB is ten times.`},{title:`Fast, slow and the floor`,widget:`soundBallistics`,intro:`The same sound through the meter's three lenses, replayed. Pick a scene. The floor follows the quiet: it drops at once and creeps up 0.5 dB a second, so a fan that keeps running becomes the new quiet.`},{title:`What the screen shows`,widget:`soundScreens`,intro:`Four views. Click = next, double-click = the action at the bottom left (speed, quiet, diary, alarm), long press = re-learn the quiet. While the alarm rings, any press silences it.`}],steps:[`Same wiring as S1. Optional: lesson 02's LED on GPIO 4 through its resistor (the alarm light).`,`Flash <code>sound-lab-s2</code> and stay quiet for a second. <b>Checkpoint 1:</b> the meter reads 0-3 dB, “quiet”.`,`Talk at a normal voice 30 cm away. <b>Checkpoint 2:</b> 15-25 dB, “talking”.`,`<code>speed slow</code>, then <code>speed fast</code>. <b>Checkpoint 3:</b> you can describe the difference.`,`Play music loudly near the mic. <b>Checkpoint 4:</b> TOO LOUD on the alarm view, then silence it with the button.`,`Leave it for ten minutes, then <code>diary</code>. <b>Checkpoint 5:</b> a table of your room's ten minutes.`],note:`dB SPL needs a microphone whose sensitivity is known: the INMP441's is in its datasheet, so S2 can show it (unweighted, about ±2 dB; real meters often show dBA, which weights the frequencies like the ear and reads lower for a deep hum). The KY-037's numbers are dB above <i>your</i> room's quiet, not dB SPL (the number a calibrated meter shows): that would need a reference microphone and the capsule's sensitivity. The floor is learnt in the first half second after boot or <code>quiet</code>, then follows the quiet (down at once, up 0.5 dB/s). Settings (speed, limit, hold, alarm) survive a reset in NVS; the diary doesn't.`,preview:{widget:`sound`}},{kind:`lesson`,id:`S3`,track:`sound`,title:`Clap control`,status:`built, not flashed`,heading:`Switch things with your hands`,goal:`<span class="snd-parts"><span class="need">KY-037 or INMP441</span><span class="opt">MAX98357A + speaker (optional)</span></span>One clap toggles the LED, two change the RGB colour, three put the screen to sleep, four throw a party. A voice, a whistle or music never counts: a clap is not just loud, it has a <b>shape</b> (a jump within 4 ms, over within 160 ms). Claps close together form one pattern; a pause of 0.65 s ends it. What each pattern does is a map in NVS you can change, and each action answers with its own little sound on the speaker.`,goalLine:`Make 1, 2, 3 and 4 claps do four different things, prove that talking doesn't trigger them, and tune the detector to your own claps.`,goalCheck:[`One clap toggles the LED on GPIO 4 (or prints <code>LED on</code>), two change the RGB colour.`,`On the <b>timeline</b>, a clap gets a teal flag, a spoken word an amber <i>long</i> bar, and nothing happens.`,`<code>learn</code> + 5 claps set <code>sens</code> from your softest clap.`,`You remapped a pattern with <code>map</code> and it survived a reset.`],learn:[`onset detection`,`a clap's shape`,`hysteresis`,`grouping in time`,`calibrating from data`,`NVS map`,`sound as feedback`,`echo guard`],pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`3V3`,idx:1,role:`MICS +`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`6`,role:`AO`,side:`L`,kind:`sound`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`5V`,idx:20,role:`AMP VIN`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`GND`,idx:0,role:`MICS G`,side:`R`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`42`,role:`SD`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-mic`},{pin:`41`,role:`WS`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-mic`},{pin:`40`,role:`SCK`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-mic`},{pin:`39`,role:`BCLK`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-amp`},{pin:`38`,role:`LRC`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-amp`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`},{pin:`47`,role:`DIN`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-amp`},{pin:`21`,role:`DO`,side:`R`,kind:`sound`},{pin:`GND`,idx:20,role:`AMP G`,side:`R`,kind:`gnd`}],boot:!0,cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/sound-lab-s3 -e sound-lab-s3 -t upload`,wiring:{title:`Wiring: the microphones and the speaker (wire what the lab needs; the screen stays as in lesson 03)`,head:[`Module pin`,`ESP32-S3`,`Notes`],rows:[[`KY-037 + · G`,`3V3`,`The analog mic. + on the second 3V3 pin (the screen has the first), G on GND. <b>Never 5 V</b>: AO and DO swing up to the module's supply, and the ESP32-S3's pins take 3.3 V at most.`],[`KY-037 AO`,`GPIO 6`,`Analog out: the microphone's voltage. GPIO 6 is ADC1 channel 5: ADC1 keeps working while Wi-Fi is on, ADC2 doesn't.`],[`KY-037 DO`,`GPIO 21`,`Digital out: the LM393 comparator says high when the sound is louder than the blue trim pot's setting.`],[`INMP441 VDD · GND`,`3V3`,`The digital mic (I2S). VDD on 3V3 (share the breadboard rail with the KY-037), GND on GND. 3.3 V only.`],[`INMP441 L/R`,`GND`,`Low = it talks in the left slot, the one the board reads.`],[`INMP441 SCK`,`GPIO 40`,`The bit clock. The ESP32 drives it: 16 000 × 64 = 1.024 MHz.`],[`INMP441 WS`,`GPIO 41`,`Word select: left slot / right slot, flips every 32 clock ticks.`],[`INMP441 SD`,`GPIO 42`,`The data, most significant bit first. Pulled down, so a missing mic reads as zeros.`],[`MAX98357A VIN · GND`,`5V`,`The speaker amplifier. 5V gives the most (about 3 W into 4 Ω); 3V3 works, quieter.`],[`MAX98357A BCLK`,`GPIO 39`,`Bit clock of the second I2S port (I2S1; the mic uses I2S0, so both run at once).`],[`MAX98357A LRC`,`GPIO 38`,`Word select (left/right).`],[`MAX98357A DIN`,`GPIO 47`,`The samples, 16-bit.`],[`MAX98357A GAIN · SD`,`nothing`,`Leave both unconnected: 9 dB gain, and it plays (left + right) / 2.`],[`speaker (4 Ω, 3 W)`,`amp + / −`,`On the amplifier's screw terminal: the JST-PH2.0 lead's two wires, red to +.`],[`screen (8 wires)`,`as lesson 03`,`Unchanged. The push button on GPIO 5 is optional: it does what BOOT does.`]]},commands:[{command:`uv run pio run -d labs/sound-lab-s3 -e sound-lab-s3 -t upload`,where:`mac`,card:`squiet`,does:`Builds lab S3 and writes it to the board, then open the monitor with <code>uv run pio device monitor</code>.`,see:`<code>Listening for an INMP441 on the I2S pins...</code>, <code>Microphone: INMP441 (I2S, digital) (mic setting: auto)</code> (or KY-037), then <code>Sound lab S3 …</code>, the command list and a three-note hello from the speaker. Stay quiet for half a second: <code>- learning the quiet...</code>`,fix:`Wrong mic picked: <code>mic inmp441</code> or <code>mic ky037</code> (saved, restarts). No hello: the speaker is optional; check VIN and DIN. “Failed to connect”: hold BOOT, tap RST, release BOOT, run it again.`},{command:`(clap once, then twice)`,where:`hands`,card:`ssound`,does:`One clap = <code>led</code>, two = <code>colour</code> (the defaults; three = <code>screen</code>, four = <code>party</code>).`,see:`<code>- 1 clap -> led</code>, <code>+ LED on</code>; then <code>- 2 claps -> colour</code>, <code>+ colour: warm white</code>. The <b>lamp</b> view shows both.`,fix:`Nothing: claps too soft for <code>sens</code> 18 dB. Try <code>learn</code>.`},{command:`test`,card:`ssound`,does:`Prints every clap, long sound and group as it happens.`,see:`<code>  clap at 5120 ms: 31.0 dB, 48 ms long</code>, <code>  LONG (not a clap) at …: 22.0 dB, 380 ms long</code>, <code>  group of 2: gaps 290 ms</code>.`,fix:`<code>test off</code> to stop.`},{command:`learn`,card:`ssound`,does:`Jumps to <b>tune</b>: clap 5 times as you normally would. sens becomes 60 % of the softest (between 8 and 40 dB), saved.`,see:`<code>- clap 1 of 5: 29 dB</code> … <code>+ learnt: sens 17 dB (saved)</code>; five bars with their dB and ms.`,fix:`Talking during learn is counted under <i>long (voice?)</i>, not as a clap.`},{command:`map 3 tune`,card:`sany`,does:`Three claps now play twinkle on the speaker. Actions: led colour dark screen view party tune none.`,see:`<code>+ 3 claps -> tune (saved)</code>; <code>maps</code> prints all four.`,fix:``},{command:`sound off`,card:`sany`,does:`Each action answers with a sound: LED up/down, a colour arpeggio, a click for screen and view, a fanfare for the party, a low No when a pattern isn't mapped. <code>sound off</code> silences them (saved).`,see:`<code>+ pattern sounds off (saved)</code>.`,fix:``},{command:`gap 400`,card:`sany`,does:`A shorter pause ends a pattern: snappier, but slow clappers split into two patterns.`,see:`<code>+ pause 400 ms ends a pattern (saved)</code>.`,fix:``},{command:`do party`,card:`any`,does:`Runs an action without clapping (to test the wiring).`,see:`<code>+ party!</code>, 2 s of rainbow on the RGB LED.`,fix:``}],panels:[{title:`Is it a clap? The detector, 4 ms at a time`,widget:`soundClapMachine`,intro:`The same rules as kit/lib/sound-kit/src/onsets.h, run on a sound you pick. Step one frame at a time or play it, move the rise and release lines. Armed → loud (ONSET) → quiet (CLAP if it was short, LONG if not) → armed again after 40 ms of quiet.`},{title:`What the screen shows`,widget:`soundScreens`,intro:`Four views. Click = next, double-click = the action at the bottom left (test, colour, maps, learn), long press = learn.`}],steps:[`Same wiring as S1. Optional: lesson 02's LED on GPIO 4.`,`Flash <code>sound-lab-s3</code>, stay quiet a second. <b>Checkpoint 1:</b> the <b>timeline</b> shows a flat line with the rise and release lines.`,`Clap once, then twice. <b>Checkpoint 2:</b> LED toggles, RGB changes colour.`,`Talk normally next to the mic. <b>Checkpoint 3:</b> amber <i>long</i> bars, no action.`,`<code>learn</code>, clap 5 times. <b>Checkpoint 4:</b> a new <code>sens</code>, saved.`,`<code>map 1 party</code>, clap once, reset the board, clap once again. <b>Checkpoint 5:</b> still a party.`],note:`The board's own sounds never count as claps: while the speaker plays (and 200 ms after) the detector gets no frames, the echo guard. Five or more claps in a row count as applause and do nothing. Three claps put the backlight out: it comes back after 30 s or with any button press, so the screen can't be lost. The detector listens to AO through the level meter, not to DO: one clap makes dozens of DO edges.`,preview:{widget:`sound`}},{kind:`lesson`,id:`S4`,track:`sound`,title:`Recorder`,status:`built, not flashed`,heading:`A voice-activated recorder`,goal:`<span class="snd-parts"><span class="need">KY-037 or INMP441</span><span class="opt">Wi-Fi (W1) (optional)</span><span class="opt">MAX98357A + speaker (optional)</span></span>Armed, the board waits for a sound louder than the trigger, then records into PSRAM, starting half a second <i>before</i> the trigger (the ring buffer still has it), until 1.2 s of quiet. Up to 12 clips, 6 MB, gone on reset. Listen on your phone at <code>http://bench.local</code> (the Wi-Fi network W1 saved, plus the admin token), or pull a WAV over USB with <code>tools/sound_pull.py</code>, or play it on the board's own speaker (<code>play 3 0.7</code>: slower and deeper). The board always shows REC and a red LED while it records, whoever started it.`,goalLine:`Record a sentence hands-free, see the pre-roll, play it on your phone, and pull one clip to your Mac as a WAV file.`,goalCheck:[`<code>arm</code>, then talk: the <b>recorder</b> view goes ARMED → REC → ARMED and <code>clips</code> lists a voice clip.`,`A door slam or a single clap starts a recording but is dropped (too short).`,`The phone page at <code>http://bench.local</code> unlocks with the token, draws the clip and plays it.`,`<code>tools/sound_pull.py</code> wrote a .wav that plays on the Mac.`,`<code>play</code> (or a double-click on <b>clips</b>) plays the picked clip on the board's speaker; <code>play 1 2</code> is a chipmunk.`],learn:[`pre-roll from a ring`,`trigger + hold`,`PSRAM clip store`,`WAV format`,`streaming responses`,`base64 + CRC-32`,`playback speed = pitch`,`tokens & visible recording`],pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`3V3`,idx:1,role:`MICS +`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`6`,role:`AO`,side:`L`,kind:`sound`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`5V`,idx:20,role:`AMP VIN`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`GND`,idx:0,role:`MICS G`,side:`R`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`42`,role:`SD`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-mic`},{pin:`41`,role:`WS`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-mic`},{pin:`40`,role:`SCK`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-mic`},{pin:`39`,role:`BCLK`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-amp`},{pin:`38`,role:`LRC`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-amp`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`},{pin:`47`,role:`DIN`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-amp`},{pin:`21`,role:`DO`,side:`R`,kind:`sound`},{pin:`GND`,idx:20,role:`AMP G`,side:`R`,kind:`gnd`}],boot:!0,cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/sound-lab-s4 -e sound-lab-s4 -t upload`,wiring:{title:`Wiring: the microphones and the speaker (wire what the lab needs; the screen stays as in lesson 03)`,head:[`Module pin`,`ESP32-S3`,`Notes`],rows:[[`KY-037 + · G`,`3V3`,`The analog mic. + on the second 3V3 pin (the screen has the first), G on GND. <b>Never 5 V</b>: AO and DO swing up to the module's supply, and the ESP32-S3's pins take 3.3 V at most.`],[`KY-037 AO`,`GPIO 6`,`Analog out: the microphone's voltage. GPIO 6 is ADC1 channel 5: ADC1 keeps working while Wi-Fi is on, ADC2 doesn't.`],[`KY-037 DO`,`GPIO 21`,`Digital out: the LM393 comparator says high when the sound is louder than the blue trim pot's setting.`],[`INMP441 VDD · GND`,`3V3`,`The digital mic (I2S). VDD on 3V3 (share the breadboard rail with the KY-037), GND on GND. 3.3 V only.`],[`INMP441 L/R`,`GND`,`Low = it talks in the left slot, the one the board reads.`],[`INMP441 SCK`,`GPIO 40`,`The bit clock. The ESP32 drives it: 16 000 × 64 = 1.024 MHz.`],[`INMP441 WS`,`GPIO 41`,`Word select: left slot / right slot, flips every 32 clock ticks.`],[`INMP441 SD`,`GPIO 42`,`The data, most significant bit first. Pulled down, so a missing mic reads as zeros.`],[`MAX98357A VIN · GND`,`5V`,`The speaker amplifier. 5V gives the most (about 3 W into 4 Ω); 3V3 works, quieter.`],[`MAX98357A BCLK`,`GPIO 39`,`Bit clock of the second I2S port (I2S1; the mic uses I2S0, so both run at once).`],[`MAX98357A LRC`,`GPIO 38`,`Word select (left/right).`],[`MAX98357A DIN`,`GPIO 47`,`The samples, 16-bit.`],[`MAX98357A GAIN · SD`,`nothing`,`Leave both unconnected: 9 dB gain, and it plays (left + right) / 2.`],[`speaker (4 Ω, 3 W)`,`amp + / −`,`On the amplifier's screw terminal: the JST-PH2.0 lead's two wires, red to +.`],[`screen (8 wires)`,`as lesson 03`,`Unchanged. The push button on GPIO 5 is optional: it does what BOOT does.`]]},commands:[{command:`uv run pio run -d labs/sound-lab-s4 -e sound-lab-s4 -t upload`,where:`mac`,card:`squiet`,does:`Builds lab S4 and writes it to the board, then open the monitor with <code>uv run pio device monitor</code>.`,see:`<code>Listening for an INMP441 on the I2S pins...</code>, <code>Microphone: INMP441 (I2S, digital) (mic setting: auto)</code> (or KY-037), then <code>Sound lab S4 …</code>, the command list and a three-note hello from the speaker. Stay quiet for half a second: <code>- learning the quiet...</code>`,fix:`Wrong mic picked: <code>mic inmp441</code> or <code>mic ky037</code> (saved, restarts). No hello: the speaker is optional; check VIN and DIN. “Failed to connect”: hold BOOT, tap RST, release BOOT, run it again.`},{command:`arm`,card:`squiet`,does:`Voice-activated: louder than <code>trigger</code> (15 dB above the quiet) starts a clip.`,see:`<code>- armed: talk and it records</code>; the <b>recorder</b> view says ARMED (teal ring).`,fix:``},{command:`(say a sentence, then wait)`,where:`hands`,card:`ssound`,does:`Records from half a second before your first word until 1.2 s after the last loud moment.`,see:`<code>x REC clip 1 (voice)</code>; REC 2.4 s in red, the header's red dot, the RGB LED red; then <code>+ clip 1: 3.1 s</code>.`,fix:`Never starts: speak closer, or <code>trigger 10</code>.`},{command:`play 1 0.7`,card:`sany`,does:`Plays clip 1 on the board's speaker at 0.7× speed (deeper, slower). Without a number: the clip picked on the <b>clips</b> view. Made louder like the download, but at most ×16. <code>stop</code> stops it; <code>rec</code> stops it before recording; it is refused while recording.`,see:`<code>+ playing clip 1 (4.4 s) at x0.70</code>, then <code>- clip 1 played</code>; waves in the header.`,fix:`<code>recording: stop first (the microphone would hear the speaker)</code>.`},{command:`clips`,card:`sany`,does:`Lists the clips: number, length, voice or hand, loudest, how much louder it is sent, when.`,see:`<code>    1    3.1s  voice  -38.2 dBFS  x23.4  2026-10-14 14:02:31</code>`,fix:``},{command:`token`,card:`sany`,does:`Prints the admin token the phone page asks for (shared with W2/R7/R8).`,see:`<code>Admin token: 3f…</code> and where to paste it.`,fix:`Keep it out of chats and git; <code>token new</code> replaces it.`},{command:`http://bench.local`,where:`phone`,card:`sany`,does:`The board's own page: live level, arm / rec / stop, the clip list with each waveform, play and download, a speed choice and <b>Play on board</b> / <b>Stop on board</b> per clip. The token stays in that tab only.`,see:`A clip card with its shape; play moves a playhead.`,fix:`“no Wi-Fi saved”: flash wifi-lab-w1 once and join. Page unreachable: same network? Try the IP from <code>wifi</code>.`},{command:`uv run python tools/sound_pull.py /dev/cu.usbserial-A5069RR4 1`,where:`mac`,card:`sany`,does:`Close the monitor first. Types <code>send 1</code>, reads the base64 WAV, checks the CRC-32 and the size, writes the file.`,see:`<code>Receiving clip 1: 99244 bytes as …</code>, then <code>Saved … (3.1 s at 16000 Hz, 99244 bytes, CRC ok)</code>.`,fix:`<code>Could not open … (is the serial monitor still open?)</code>: close it.`}],panels:[{title:`Voice-activated recording, step by step`,widget:`soundRecorderTimeline`,intro:`Nine seconds: a door bang, then a sentence with a pause in it. Step through or play, move the trigger and the hold. Amber = the pre-roll taken from the ring, red = recorded, grey = dropped.`},{title:`Getting the audio out`,widget:`soundGetOut`,intro:`Three paths: the phone over Wi-Fi, the Mac over the USB serial port, or the board's own speaker. Below: the 44 bytes every WAV file starts with (for a 3.2 s clip at 16 000 Hz).`},{title:`What the screen shows`,widget:`soundScreens`,intro:`Three views. Click = next, double-click = the action at the bottom left (arm, next, wifi), long press = rec / stop.`}],steps:[`Same wiring as S1. The board needs the Wi-Fi network W1 saved (only for the phone page).`,`Flash <code>sound-lab-s4</code>. <b>Checkpoint 1:</b> OFF on the recorder view and <code>page: http://bench.local</code> once Wi-Fi joins.`,`<code>arm</code>, say a sentence. <b>Checkpoint 2:</b> one clip; the amber pre-roll before your first word.`,`Clap once. <b>Checkpoint 3:</b> <code>a bang, not a voice: clip 2 dropped</code>.`,`Open the page on your phone, paste the <code>token</code>. <b>Checkpoint 4:</b> your clip plays.`,`Close the monitor, run <code>tools/sound_pull.py</code>. <b>Checkpoint 5:</b> a .wav on your Mac.`],note:`Privacy is built in, not optional: recording always shows on the board (REC, red dot, red LED) whoever starts it; every audio route on the page needs the admin token; clips live in RAM only and vanish on reset. The sound will be thin and hissy: the clips are normalised (made up to 64 times louder) on the way out, and that lifts the hiss with the voice. This module is a loudness sensor first, a microphone second.`,preview:{widget:`sound`}},{kind:`lesson`,id:`S5`,track:`sound`,title:`Spectrum`,status:`built, not flashed`,heading:`See which frequencies are in a sound`,goal:`<span class="snd-parts"><span class="need">KY-037 or INMP441</span><span class="opt">MAX98357A + speaker (optional)</span></span>Our own FFT (no library) runs on the newest 512 samples about 25 times a second: bars from 100 Hz to 8 kHz, a scrolling waterfall, a tuner that names the note you whistle or hum, and a game you steer with the pitch of your whistle. With the speaker, the board checks itself: <code>ref</code> plays a reference note for the tuner, <code>sweep</code> climbs from 100 Hz to 8 kHz across the bars.`,goalLine:`Find the frequency of your whistle, see why finer bins are slower, and score in the steer game.`,goalCheck:[`The <b>bars</b> view shows a single tall bar when you whistle and many low ones when you talk.`,`The <b>tuner</b> names your note and the needle goes teal within ±5 cents.`,`<code>fft 1024</code> and <code>fft 256</code>: you can say what changed (Hz per bin, how fast it reacts).`,`<b>steer</b>: you scored at least 3 targets.`],learn:[`FFT bins`,`Hann window & leakage`,`Nyquist (8 kHz)`,`resolution vs speed`,`log frequency`,`notes & cents`,`reference tones`,`log sweep`],pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`3V3`,idx:1,role:`MICS +`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`6`,role:`AO`,side:`L`,kind:`sound`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`5V`,idx:20,role:`AMP VIN`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`GND`,idx:0,role:`MICS G`,side:`R`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`42`,role:`SD`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-mic`},{pin:`41`,role:`WS`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-mic`},{pin:`40`,role:`SCK`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-mic`},{pin:`39`,role:`BCLK`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-amp`},{pin:`38`,role:`LRC`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-amp`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`},{pin:`47`,role:`DIN`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-amp`},{pin:`21`,role:`DO`,side:`R`,kind:`sound`},{pin:`GND`,idx:20,role:`AMP G`,side:`R`,kind:`gnd`}],boot:!0,cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/sound-lab-s5 -e sound-lab-s5 -t upload`,wiring:{title:`Wiring: the microphones and the speaker (wire what the lab needs; the screen stays as in lesson 03)`,head:[`Module pin`,`ESP32-S3`,`Notes`],rows:[[`KY-037 + · G`,`3V3`,`The analog mic. + on the second 3V3 pin (the screen has the first), G on GND. <b>Never 5 V</b>: AO and DO swing up to the module's supply, and the ESP32-S3's pins take 3.3 V at most.`],[`KY-037 AO`,`GPIO 6`,`Analog out: the microphone's voltage. GPIO 6 is ADC1 channel 5: ADC1 keeps working while Wi-Fi is on, ADC2 doesn't.`],[`KY-037 DO`,`GPIO 21`,`Digital out: the LM393 comparator says high when the sound is louder than the blue trim pot's setting.`],[`INMP441 VDD · GND`,`3V3`,`The digital mic (I2S). VDD on 3V3 (share the breadboard rail with the KY-037), GND on GND. 3.3 V only.`],[`INMP441 L/R`,`GND`,`Low = it talks in the left slot, the one the board reads.`],[`INMP441 SCK`,`GPIO 40`,`The bit clock. The ESP32 drives it: 16 000 × 64 = 1.024 MHz.`],[`INMP441 WS`,`GPIO 41`,`Word select: left slot / right slot, flips every 32 clock ticks.`],[`INMP441 SD`,`GPIO 42`,`The data, most significant bit first. Pulled down, so a missing mic reads as zeros.`],[`MAX98357A VIN · GND`,`5V`,`The speaker amplifier. 5V gives the most (about 3 W into 4 Ω); 3V3 works, quieter.`],[`MAX98357A BCLK`,`GPIO 39`,`Bit clock of the second I2S port (I2S1; the mic uses I2S0, so both run at once).`],[`MAX98357A LRC`,`GPIO 38`,`Word select (left/right).`],[`MAX98357A DIN`,`GPIO 47`,`The samples, 16-bit.`],[`MAX98357A GAIN · SD`,`nothing`,`Leave both unconnected: 9 dB gain, and it plays (left + right) / 2.`],[`speaker (4 Ω, 3 W)`,`amp + / −`,`On the amplifier's screw terminal: the JST-PH2.0 lead's two wires, red to +.`],[`screen (8 wires)`,`as lesson 03`,`Unchanged. The push button on GPIO 5 is optional: it does what BOOT does.`]]},commands:[{command:`uv run pio run -d labs/sound-lab-s5 -e sound-lab-s5 -t upload`,where:`mac`,card:`squiet`,does:`Builds lab S5 and writes it to the board, then open the monitor with <code>uv run pio device monitor</code>.`,see:`<code>Listening for an INMP441 on the I2S pins...</code>, <code>Microphone: INMP441 (I2S, digital) (mic setting: auto)</code> (or KY-037), then <code>Sound lab S5 …</code>, the command list and a three-note hello from the speaker. Stay quiet for half a second: <code>- learning the quiet...</code>`,fix:`Wrong mic picked: <code>mic inmp441</code> or <code>mic ky037</code> (saved, restarts). No hello: the speaker is optional; check VIN and DIN. “Failed to connect”: hold BOOT, tap RST, release BOOT, run it again.`},{command:`(whistle, then hum)`,where:`hands`,card:`ssound`,does:`The <b>bars</b> view: one tall bar for a whistle (1-3 kHz), lower and wider for a hum.`,see:`<code>loudest 1.76 kHz (A6)</code> at the top; right: <code>N 512 - 31 Hz/bin - … us</code>.`,fix:`Nothing moves: whistle within a hand's width (no amplifier on the module).`},{command:`peaks`,card:`ssound`,does:`Prints the 5 strongest frequencies with their notes.`,see:`<code>   1765.6 Hz   -31.2 dBFS  A6   +2 cents</code> …`,fix:``},{command:`fft 1024`,card:`sany`,does:`Twice as many samples: bins 15.6 Hz apart, but each answer looks at 64 ms. <code>fft 256</code>: 62.5 Hz bins, 16 ms.`,see:`<code>- FFT 1024: 15.6 Hz per bin, 64 ms window</code>.`,fix:``},{command:`ref (or ref A4, ref 440)`,card:`squiet`,does:`A 1 s sine on the speaker: the note nearest to what you whistled in the last 2 s, else A4. S5 reads the ring directly, so it hears its own tone on purpose. Double-click on <b>tuner</b> does the same.`,see:`<code>- reference A4 = 440.0 Hz, 1 s</code>; the tuner shows A4 with the needle near 0.`,fix:`Off by many cents: the speaker and mic are fine; check <code>a4</code>.`},{command:`sweep`,card:`squiet`,does:`A 4 s tone from 100 Hz to 8 kHz on a log scale; the screen jumps to the waterfall.`,see:`A diagonal line climbing the waterfall at an even slope: log frequency, like the bars.`,fix:``},{command:`a4 442`,card:`sany`,does:`Tunes the note names to A4 = 442 Hz (some orchestras do).`,see:`<code>- A4 = 442.0 Hz</code>; the tuner's cents shift by about 8.`,fix:``},{command:`(steer view: whistle up and down)`,where:`hands`,card:`ssound`,does:`The dot follows your pitch on a log scale (600-2500 Hz); hold it in the ring 1 s to score. The RGB LED takes the dot's colour.`,see:`<code>+ target 1!</code>, a new ring elsewhere.`,fix:`Can't reach the top: <code>range 500 2000</code>.`},{command:`limits`,card:`sany`,does:`What this FFT can and can't see.`,see:`Up to 8000 Hz (Nyquist), bins 31.2 Hz apart, 32 ms per answer, and the no-amplifier warning.`,fix:``}],panels:[{title:`Build a sound, see its spectrum`,widget:`soundFftExplainer`,intro:`Up to three tones plus noise, the samples on top and the FFT's bins below. Untick the window to see leakage; change N to trade sharp bins for a slower answer.`},{title:`From a whistle to a note`,widget:`soundWhistleSeq`,intro:`Six steps, the same as the tuner view does 25 times a second. Move the whistle slider and watch the parabola find the pitch between two bins.`},{title:`What the screen shows`,widget:`soundScreens`,intro:`Four views. Click = next, double-click = the action at the bottom left (hold, hold, peaks, target), long press = hold.`}],steps:[`Same wiring as S1.`,`Flash <code>sound-lab-s5</code>. <b>Checkpoint 1:</b> the limits printed at boot.`,`Whistle a steady note on <b>bars</b>, then <b>waterfall</b>. <b>Checkpoint 2:</b> one bright line on the waterfall.`,`<b>tuner</b>: whistle until the needle is teal. <b>Checkpoint 3:</b> a note name you can repeat.`,`<code>fft 256</code>, <code>fft 1024</code>, back to <code>fft 512</code>. <b>Checkpoint 4:</b> you saw the trade-off.`,`<b>steer</b>: score 3. <b>Checkpoint 5:</b> <code>target 3!</code>.`],note:`Limits worth knowing: at 16 000 samples a second nothing above 8 kHz can be seen (Nyquist), and higher sounds fold back as fakes. Each band learns its own quiet (a fan hums low, a laptop whines high), so the views show dB above each band's floor. Speech has many harmonics and no single clear peak, which is why the tuner wants clarity ≥ 12 dB: whistle or hum.`,preview:{widget:`sound`}},{kind:`lesson`,id:`S6`,track:`sound`,title:`Sound games`,status:`built, not flashed`,heading:`Play with what the microphone hears`,goal:`<span class="snd-parts"><span class="need">KY-037 or INMP441</span><span class="opt">MAX98357A + speaker (optional)</span></span>Three games on one microphone, each listening only while its view is on screen. <b>knock</b>: a knock lock that opens for your secret rhythm, fast or slow. <b>bird</b>: Clap Bird, every clap is a flap. <b>party</b>: the board finds the beat in music and throws light at it. With the speaker: blips, dings and a crash in Clap Bird, the secret knock played back, an unlock tune.`,goalLine:`Open the lock with your secret knock (and fail with a wrong one), get past 5 pipes in Clap Bird, and make the party follow a song's tempo.`,goalCheck:[`<code>knock set</code> + a rhythm saved a secret; the same rhythm slower still opens (LED on GPIO 4, green RGB for 5 s).`,`A different rhythm shakes the padlock: <code>locked: the rhythm is off</code>.`,`Clap Bird: a score of 5 or more, and <code>best</code> survives a reset.`,`<b>party</b> shows a BPM close to the song's tempo.`],learn:[`tempo-free rhythm`,`tolerance`,`fixed-step game physics`,`latency (onset vs clap)`,`beat detection`,`median tempo`,`one sensor, many uses`],pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`3V3`,idx:1,role:`MICS +`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`6`,role:`AO`,side:`L`,kind:`sound`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`5V`,idx:20,role:`AMP VIN`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`GND`,idx:0,role:`MICS G`,side:`R`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`42`,role:`SD`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-mic`},{pin:`41`,role:`WS`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-mic`},{pin:`40`,role:`SCK`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-mic`},{pin:`39`,role:`BCLK`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-amp`},{pin:`38`,role:`LRC`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-amp`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`},{pin:`47`,role:`DIN`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-amp`},{pin:`21`,role:`DO`,side:`R`,kind:`sound`},{pin:`GND`,idx:20,role:`AMP G`,side:`R`,kind:`gnd`}],boot:!0,cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/sound-lab-s6 -e sound-lab-s6 -t upload`,wiring:{title:`Wiring: the microphones and the speaker (wire what the lab needs; the screen stays as in lesson 03)`,head:[`Module pin`,`ESP32-S3`,`Notes`],rows:[[`KY-037 + · G`,`3V3`,`The analog mic. + on the second 3V3 pin (the screen has the first), G on GND. <b>Never 5 V</b>: AO and DO swing up to the module's supply, and the ESP32-S3's pins take 3.3 V at most.`],[`KY-037 AO`,`GPIO 6`,`Analog out: the microphone's voltage. GPIO 6 is ADC1 channel 5: ADC1 keeps working while Wi-Fi is on, ADC2 doesn't.`],[`KY-037 DO`,`GPIO 21`,`Digital out: the LM393 comparator says high when the sound is louder than the blue trim pot's setting.`],[`INMP441 VDD · GND`,`3V3`,`The digital mic (I2S). VDD on 3V3 (share the breadboard rail with the KY-037), GND on GND. 3.3 V only.`],[`INMP441 L/R`,`GND`,`Low = it talks in the left slot, the one the board reads.`],[`INMP441 SCK`,`GPIO 40`,`The bit clock. The ESP32 drives it: 16 000 × 64 = 1.024 MHz.`],[`INMP441 WS`,`GPIO 41`,`Word select: left slot / right slot, flips every 32 clock ticks.`],[`INMP441 SD`,`GPIO 42`,`The data, most significant bit first. Pulled down, so a missing mic reads as zeros.`],[`MAX98357A VIN · GND`,`5V`,`The speaker amplifier. 5V gives the most (about 3 W into 4 Ω); 3V3 works, quieter.`],[`MAX98357A BCLK`,`GPIO 39`,`Bit clock of the second I2S port (I2S1; the mic uses I2S0, so both run at once).`],[`MAX98357A LRC`,`GPIO 38`,`Word select (left/right).`],[`MAX98357A DIN`,`GPIO 47`,`The samples, 16-bit.`],[`MAX98357A GAIN · SD`,`nothing`,`Leave both unconnected: 9 dB gain, and it plays (left + right) / 2.`],[`speaker (4 Ω, 3 W)`,`amp + / −`,`On the amplifier's screw terminal: the JST-PH2.0 lead's two wires, red to +.`],[`screen (8 wires)`,`as lesson 03`,`Unchanged. The push button on GPIO 5 is optional: it does what BOOT does.`]]},commands:[{command:`uv run pio run -d labs/sound-lab-s6 -e sound-lab-s6 -t upload`,where:`mac`,card:`squiet`,does:`Builds lab S6 and writes it to the board, then open the monitor with <code>uv run pio device monitor</code>.`,see:`<code>Listening for an INMP441 on the I2S pins...</code>, <code>Microphone: INMP441 (I2S, digital) (mic setting: auto)</code> (or KY-037), then <code>Sound lab S6 …</code>, the command list and a three-note hello from the speaker. Stay quiet for half a second: <code>- learning the quiet...</code>`,fix:`Wrong mic picked: <code>mic inmp441</code> or <code>mic ky037</code> (saved, restarts). No hello: the speaker is optional; check VIN and DIN. “Failed to connect”: hold BOOT, tap RST, release BOOT, run it again.`},{command:`knock set`,card:`squiet`,does:`The next pattern becomes the secret (2-12 knocks; 1.5 s of quiet ends it). Saved in NVS.`,see:`<code>- knock your new secret, then wait 1.5 s</code>, then <code>+ secret saved: 5 knocks, 1.2 s</code>.`,fix:`<code>a secret needs 2 knocks or more</code>.`},{command:`(knock the secret on the table)`,where:`hands`,card:`ssound`,does:`Every finished pattern is compared with the secret.`,see:`<code>+ OPEN (score 92)</code>: the shackle lifts, LED on GPIO 4 and the RGB LED green for 5 s. Wrong: <code>x locked: the rhythm is off</code>.`,fix:`Knocks missed: knock firmly, near the mic; <code>knock show</code> prints both rhythms.`},{command:`knock play`,card:`squiet`,does:`Plays the secret back as clicks at the knocks' exact times (an esp_timer checks every 2 ms).`,see:`<code>- playing the secret: 5 knocks</code> and your rhythm from the speaker.`,fix:`<code>sound is off: sound on</code>.`},{command:`tol 20`,card:`sany`,does:`Each gap may be off by 20 % of the pattern (default 12).`,see:`<code>- tolerance 20%</code>.`,fix:``},{command:`bird`,card:`ssound`,does:`Clap Bird: a new round. A clap starts it; every clap flaps.`,see:`<code>clap to fly!</code>; later <code>+ new best: 6</code>.`,fix:`Flaps late: knock-style claps (short and sharp) fire the onset fastest.`},{command:`sound off`,card:`sany`,does:`Game sounds on the speaker off (saved in NVS, namespace <code>games</code>).`,see:`<code>- game sounds off</code>.`,fix:``},{command:`party click on`,card:`smusic`,does:`A tick on every beat. The board can't hear for about 200 ms after each tick (the echo guard), so beats in that window are skipped. Off by default.`,see:`<code>- a tick on every beat (the board can't hear for ~200 ms after it)</code>.`,fix:``},{command:`(play music near the mic)`,where:`hands`,card:`smusic`,does:`On <b>party</b>, every beat flashes a colour, sends out a ring and lights the RGB LED.`,see:`A BPM number (e.g. 120) after a few beats.`,fix:`Too many beats: <code>party sens 2.0</code>. Too few: <code>party sens 1.3</code>.`}],panels:[{title:`The knock lock's matcher`,widget:`soundKnockMatcher`,intro:`Knock with the button (or Space). The first pattern becomes the secret; the next ones are compared gap by gap, each gap as a share of the whole pattern, so speed doesn't matter. Move the tolerance.`},{title:`What the screen shows`,widget:`soundScreens`,intro:`Three views. Click = next, double-click = the action at the bottom left (knock set, bird, party reset), long press = knock show. In this preview, a click on the screen (or Space) is a clap: Clap Bird is playable.`}],steps:[`Same wiring as S1. Optional: lesson 02's LED on GPIO 4 as the lock's bolt.`,`Flash <code>sound-lab-s6</code>. On <b>knock</b>: <code>knock set</code> and knock a rhythm. <b>Checkpoint 1:</b> secret saved.`,`Knock it again, a bit slower. <b>Checkpoint 2:</b> OPEN. Then a wrong one: locked.`,`Click to <b>bird</b> and clap. <b>Checkpoint 3:</b> past 5 pipes.`,`Click to <b>party</b>, play a song. <b>Checkpoint 4:</b> the BPM settles.`],note:`Knocks are claps to the detector (short bangs, up to 200 ms here). The lock compares rhythm, not loudness or speed, so it is a toy, not security: anyone who hears you knock can copy it. Clap Bird runs its physics in fixed 1/60 s steps whatever the screen does, so the game feels the same on any frame rate; it flaps on the onset (at once), not on the finished clap (160 ms later).`,preview:{widget:`sound`}},{kind:`lesson`,id:`S7`,track:`sound`,title:`Speaker`,status:`built, not flashed`,heading:`Make the board play: a synth on one speaker`,goal:`<span class="snd-parts"><span class="need">MAX98357A + speaker</span></span>Numbers go out over I2S to the <b>MAX98357A</b>, a class-D amplifier: it switches the speaker fully on and off about 300 000 times a second, and how long each pulse lasts follows the number. The cone is too slow to follow, so it moves with the average: the wave. S7 makes notes in five timbres, chords, envelopes, tunes and game sounds, and draws what you hear.`,goalLine:`Hear the difference between the five waves, play a chord and a tune, change the envelope until a note plucks, and fire every game sound.`,goalCheck:[`<code>beep</code> and the hello chime come out of the speaker at <code>vol 20</code>.`,`On <b>wave</b>, the spectrum shows one line for sine, odd harmonics for square, all for saw.`,`<code>adsr 1 300 0 50</code> makes <code>note A4 600</code> sound plucked; <code>adsr 300 80 65 300</code> soft.`,`<code>tune korobeiniki</code> plays and the piano roll scrolls; <code>fx explosion</code> rumbles.`],learn:[`I2S out`,`class-D amplifier`,`timbre = harmonics`,`aliasing at 16 kHz`,`ADSR vs clicks`,`semitones & chords`,`tempo`,`procedural sound`,`squared volume`],pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`3V3`,idx:1,role:`MICS +`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`6`,role:`AO`,side:`L`,kind:`sound`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`5V`,idx:20,role:`AMP VIN`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`GND`,idx:0,role:`MICS G`,side:`R`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`42`,role:`SD`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-mic`},{pin:`41`,role:`WS`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-mic`},{pin:`40`,role:`SCK`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-mic`},{pin:`39`,role:`BCLK`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-amp`},{pin:`38`,role:`LRC`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-amp`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`},{pin:`47`,role:`DIN`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-amp`},{pin:`21`,role:`DO`,side:`R`,kind:`sound`},{pin:`GND`,idx:20,role:`AMP G`,side:`R`,kind:`gnd`}],boot:!0,cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/sound-lab-s7 -e sound-lab-s7 -t upload`,wiring:{title:`Wiring: the microphones and the speaker (wire what the lab needs; the screen stays as in lesson 03)`,head:[`Module pin`,`ESP32-S3`,`Notes`],rows:[[`KY-037 + · G`,`3V3`,`The analog mic. + on the second 3V3 pin (the screen has the first), G on GND. <b>Never 5 V</b>: AO and DO swing up to the module's supply, and the ESP32-S3's pins take 3.3 V at most.`],[`KY-037 AO`,`GPIO 6`,`Analog out: the microphone's voltage. GPIO 6 is ADC1 channel 5: ADC1 keeps working while Wi-Fi is on, ADC2 doesn't.`],[`KY-037 DO`,`GPIO 21`,`Digital out: the LM393 comparator says high when the sound is louder than the blue trim pot's setting.`],[`INMP441 VDD · GND`,`3V3`,`The digital mic (I2S). VDD on 3V3 (share the breadboard rail with the KY-037), GND on GND. 3.3 V only.`],[`INMP441 L/R`,`GND`,`Low = it talks in the left slot, the one the board reads.`],[`INMP441 SCK`,`GPIO 40`,`The bit clock. The ESP32 drives it: 16 000 × 64 = 1.024 MHz.`],[`INMP441 WS`,`GPIO 41`,`Word select: left slot / right slot, flips every 32 clock ticks.`],[`INMP441 SD`,`GPIO 42`,`The data, most significant bit first. Pulled down, so a missing mic reads as zeros.`],[`MAX98357A VIN · GND`,`5V`,`The speaker amplifier. 5V gives the most (about 3 W into 4 Ω); 3V3 works, quieter.`],[`MAX98357A BCLK`,`GPIO 39`,`Bit clock of the second I2S port (I2S1; the mic uses I2S0, so both run at once).`],[`MAX98357A LRC`,`GPIO 38`,`Word select (left/right).`],[`MAX98357A DIN`,`GPIO 47`,`The samples, 16-bit.`],[`MAX98357A GAIN · SD`,`nothing`,`Leave both unconnected: 9 dB gain, and it plays (left + right) / 2.`],[`speaker (4 Ω, 3 W)`,`amp + / −`,`On the amplifier's screw terminal: the JST-PH2.0 lead's two wires, red to +.`],[`screen (8 wires)`,`as lesson 03`,`Unchanged. The push button on GPIO 5 is optional: it does what BOOT does.`]]},commands:[{command:`uv run pio run -d labs/sound-lab-s7 -e sound-lab-s7 -t upload`,where:`mac`,card:`squiet`,does:`Builds lab S7 and writes it to the board, then open the monitor with <code>uv run pio device monitor</code>.`,see:`<code>Listening for an INMP441 on the I2S pins...</code>, <code>Microphone: INMP441 (I2S, digital) (mic setting: auto)</code> (or KY-037), then <code>Sound lab S7 …</code>, the command list and a three-note hello from the speaker. Stay quiet for half a second: <code>- learning the quiet...</code>`,fix:`Wrong mic picked: <code>mic inmp441</code> or <code>mic ky037</code> (saved, restarts). No hello: the speaker is optional; check VIN and DIN. “Failed to connect”: hold BOOT, tap RST, release BOOT, run it again.`},{command:`vol 20`,card:`squiet`,does:`Start quiet: volume is squared, 20 = 4 % of full power. Saved in NVS (default 40).`,see:`<code>- volume 20</code>; then <code>beep</code> plays 440 Hz.`,fix:`Silent: VIN to 5V, DIN to 47, BCLK 39, LRC 38; the speaker on the terminal.`},{command:`wave square, then wave saw`,card:`squiet`,does:`The timbre: sine, square, triangle, saw, noise (<code>wave next</code>, or a double-click on <b>wave</b>).`,see:`<code>- wave square</code>; the spectrum's lines at 1, 3, 5… × the note.`,fix:``},{command:`note A4 600`,card:`squiet`,does:`One note (a name or Hz) for 600 ms. A double-click on <b>envelope</b> does the same.`,see:`The dot rides the ADSR curve.`,fix:`<code>close to 8 kHz: the limit at 16 kHz</code> above 7 kHz.`},{command:`adsr 1 300 0 50`,card:`squiet`,does:`The envelope: attack ms, decay ms, sustain %, release ms (default 6 80 65 90). Attack 0 makes a click.`,see:`<code>- adsr 1 300 0 50</code>: a plucked string.`,fix:``},{command:`chord Am (G7, Fmaj7, Bdim, Dsus4)`,card:`squiet`,does:`Notes together on a one-voice speaker: the board adds them into one buffer (30 s in PSRAM).`,see:`<code>- Am = A4 C5 E5</code>; three keys light on <b>keys</b>.`,fix:``},{command:`play C4 E4 G4/2 r/4 C5`,card:`squiet`,does:`Notes one after another at <code>bpm</code> (default 120): /4 quarter, /8 eighth, r rest, . dotted.`,see:`The keys light in turn.`,fix:``},{command:`tune korobeiniki`,card:`squiet`,does:`twinkle ode happy korobeiniki scale arpeggio. Double-click on <b>tunes</b> plays the picked one; long press picks the next.`,see:`The piano roll scrolls past the amber playhead.`,fix:``},{command:`fx laser (coin jump powerup explosion siren modem)`,card:`squiet`,does:`Game sounds from recipes: sweeps, noise, a closing filter.`,see:`The whole waveform on <b>fx</b>, a playhead crossing it.`,fix:``},{command:`stop`,card:`sany`,does:`Silence.`,see:`The waves in the header stop.`,fix:``}],panels:[{title:`From numbers to a moving cone`,widget:`soundSpeakerPath`,intro:`What happens to a sample after the board computes it, why the volume knob is squared, and the echo guard that keeps the microphone from hearing the speaker as claps.`},{title:`A synth to play with`,widget:`soundSynth`,intro:`The same waves and envelope as the board. Pick a wave, press a key to hear it in this browser (quietly; nothing plays until you press), and drag the envelope's handles. The red bars are harmonics the board can't make at 16 kHz: they fold back as wrong notes.`},{title:`What the screen shows`,widget:`soundScreens`,intro:`Five views. Click = next, double-click = the action at the bottom left. Keys: long press = the next chord. Tunes and fx: long press picks the next one. Elsewhere a long press stops the sound.`}],steps:[`Wire the MAX98357A: VIN → 5V, GND, BCLK → 39, LRC → 38, DIN → 47; the speaker on its screw terminal (red to +). GAIN and SD stay unconnected. No microphone needed.`,`Flash <code>sound-lab-s7</code>. <b>Checkpoint 1:</b> the hello chime and <code>start quiet: vol 20, then raise it</code>.`,`<code>vol 20</code>, then <code>wave next</code> five times with <code>note A4</code>. <b>Checkpoint 2:</b> five timbres, five spectra.`,`<code>adsr 1 300 0 50</code>, <code>note A4 600</code>; then <code>adsr 0 0 100 0</code>. <b>Checkpoint 3:</b> you hear the click the envelope removes.`,`<code>chord Am</code>, <code>tune ode</code>, <code>fx coin</code>. <b>Checkpoint 4:</b> all three play.`],note:`One speaker, one stream: chords and tunes are rendered into PSRAM first (up to 30 s), then played. At 16 000 samples a second nothing above 8 kHz exists, so a square or saw's high harmonics fold back down as wrong frequencies: a slight grit on high notes, the honest limit of a simple synth. Every other sound lab uses the same speaker for its chimes, and the echo guard keeps its detectors deaf while it plays.`,preview:{widget:`sound`}},{kind:`lesson`,id:`S8`,track:`sound`,title:`Voice FX`,status:`built, not flashed`,heading:`A parrot that repeats you, and a live robot voice`,goal:`<span class="snd-parts"><span class="need">INMP441</span><span class="need">MAX98357A + speaker</span></span>Microphone in, speaker out. Say something and the parrot says it back: normal, as a <b>chipmunk</b> (1.5× faster), a <b>giant</b> (0.7×), a <b>robot</b> (ring modulator + 6-bit crunch), with an <b>echo</b>, or <b>reverse</b>. Then live effects: an echo you talk into, a robot voice. The parrot is voice-activated like S4 (12 dB, 0.3 s pre-roll, 0.7 s hold, 6 s at most) and never hears itself.`,goalLine:`Make the parrot repeat you in three voices, talk through the live echo without a howl, and explain why a chipmunk talks fast.`,goalCheck:[`You said a word and the parrot answered in the chipmunk voice; <code>fx giant</code> + <code>again</code> answered deep and slow.`,`<b>echo</b> view: <code>echo on</code>, a clap comes back as fading repeats, latency about 60-80 ms.`,`You provoked (and the guard stopped) a howl by pointing the speaker at the mic: <code>Howl! Move the speaker away or turn it down</code>.`],learn:[`resampling = pitch + speed`,`ring modulation f±c`,`bitcrush`,`delay + feedback`,`loop gain < 1`,`howl detection`,`latency budget`,`echo guard`],pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`3V3`,idx:1,role:`MICS +`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`6`,role:`AO`,side:`L`,kind:`sound`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`5V`,idx:20,role:`AMP VIN`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`GND`,idx:0,role:`MICS G`,side:`R`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`42`,role:`SD`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-mic`},{pin:`41`,role:`WS`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-mic`},{pin:`40`,role:`SCK`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-mic`},{pin:`39`,role:`BCLK`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-amp`},{pin:`38`,role:`LRC`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-amp`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`},{pin:`47`,role:`DIN`,side:`R`,kind:`snd-amp`},{pin:`21`,role:`DO`,side:`R`,kind:`sound`},{pin:`GND`,idx:20,role:`AMP G`,side:`R`,kind:`gnd`}],boot:!0,cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/sound-lab-s8 -e sound-lab-s8 -t upload`,wiring:{title:`Wiring: the microphones and the speaker (wire what the lab needs; the screen stays as in lesson 03)`,head:[`Module pin`,`ESP32-S3`,`Notes`],rows:[[`KY-037 + · G`,`3V3`,`The analog mic. + on the second 3V3 pin (the screen has the first), G on GND. <b>Never 5 V</b>: AO and DO swing up to the module's supply, and the ESP32-S3's pins take 3.3 V at most.`],[`KY-037 AO`,`GPIO 6`,`Analog out: the microphone's voltage. GPIO 6 is ADC1 channel 5: ADC1 keeps working while Wi-Fi is on, ADC2 doesn't.`],[`KY-037 DO`,`GPIO 21`,`Digital out: the LM393 comparator says high when the sound is louder than the blue trim pot's setting.`],[`INMP441 VDD · GND`,`3V3`,`The digital mic (I2S). VDD on 3V3 (share the breadboard rail with the KY-037), GND on GND. 3.3 V only.`],[`INMP441 L/R`,`GND`,`Low = it talks in the left slot, the one the board reads.`],[`INMP441 SCK`,`GPIO 40`,`The bit clock. The ESP32 drives it: 16 000 × 64 = 1.024 MHz.`],[`INMP441 WS`,`GPIO 41`,`Word select: left slot / right slot, flips every 32 clock ticks.`],[`INMP441 SD`,`GPIO 42`,`The data, most significant bit first. Pulled down, so a missing mic reads as zeros.`],[`MAX98357A VIN · GND`,`5V`,`The speaker amplifier. 5V gives the most (about 3 W into 4 Ω); 3V3 works, quieter.`],[`MAX98357A BCLK`,`GPIO 39`,`Bit clock of the second I2S port (I2S1; the mic uses I2S0, so both run at once).`],[`MAX98357A LRC`,`GPIO 38`,`Word select (left/right).`],[`MAX98357A DIN`,`GPIO 47`,`The samples, 16-bit.`],[`MAX98357A GAIN · SD`,`nothing`,`Leave both unconnected: 9 dB gain, and it plays (left + right) / 2.`],[`speaker (4 Ω, 3 W)`,`amp + / −`,`On the amplifier's screw terminal: the JST-PH2.0 lead's two wires, red to +.`],[`screen (8 wires)`,`as lesson 03`,`Unchanged. The push button on GPIO 5 is optional: it does what BOOT does.`]]},commands:[{command:`uv run pio run -d labs/sound-lab-s8 -e sound-lab-s8 -t upload`,where:`mac`,card:`squiet`,does:`Builds lab S8 and writes it to the board, then open the monitor with <code>uv run pio device monitor</code>.`,see:`<code>Listening for an INMP441 on the I2S pins...</code>, <code>Microphone: INMP441 (I2S, digital) (mic setting: auto)</code> (or KY-037), then <code>Sound lab S8 …</code>, the command list and a three-note hello from the speaker. Stay quiet for half a second: <code>- learning the quiet...</code>`,fix:`Wrong mic picked: <code>mic inmp441</code> or <code>mic ky037</code> (saved, restarts). No hello: the speaker is optional; check VIN and DIN. “Failed to connect”: hold BOOT, tap RST, release BOOT, run it again.`},{command:`vol 25`,card:`squiet`,does:`Keep it low: the mic hears the speaker.`,see:`<code>- volume 25</code>.`,fix:`With the KY-037: <code>KY-037: it works, but an INMP441 sounds much clearer</code>.`},{command:`(say “hello parrot”)`,where:`hands`,card:`ssound`,does:`Louder than 12 dB above the quiet starts a recording with 0.3 s from before; 0.7 s of quiet ends it; then it plays back through the voice.`,see:`The bubble: <code>I'm listening...</code>, REC, then <code>Squawk!</code> with the beak moving; <code>+ chipmunk, 1.4 s, x9 louder</code>.`,fix:`<code>too short: a bang, not a word</code>: speak a little longer. Never starts: <code>trigger 8</code>.`},{command:`fx next (or fx robot, fx reverse)`,card:`sany`,does:`The parrot's voice: normal chipmunk giant robot echo reverse. Double-click on <b>parrot</b>.`,see:`<code>- voice: robot (ring modulator + bitcrush)</code>.`,fix:``},{command:`again`,card:`squiet`,does:`Says the last recording again with the current voice (double-click on <b>scope</b>).`,see:`The same words, another voice.`,fix:`<code>nothing recorded yet: say something first</code>.`},{command:`echo on`,card:`ssound`,does:`Live: mic → delay line → speaker. <code>delay 120</code> (20-500 ms), <code>feedback 60</code> (0-70 %). Needs the mic at 16 000 Hz.`,see:`<code>! live echo: speaker away from the mic, volume low</code>; the taps of one clap on the <b>echo</b> view and a latency of ~60-80 ms.`,fix:`A howl: the guard stops it with a low No. Turn the speaker away, lower <code>vol</code> or <code>feedback</code>.`},{command:`robot on`,card:`ssound`,does:`Live: your voice × a slow sine (<code>carrier 50</code>, 10-300 Hz) + crunch. Every frequency f becomes f − c and f + c.`,see:`The robot's mouth lights with what comes out. <code>carrier 200</code> sounds like a bell.`,fix:``}],panels:[{title:`The parrot, in your browser`,widget:`soundParrot`,intro:`The same effects as fx.h, in this page. An example voice is loaded; recording your own is optional and only starts when you press the button (3 s, a red indicator while it records, nothing leaves the page).`},{title:`What the screen shows`,widget:`soundScreens`,intro:`Four views. Click = next, double-click = the action at the bottom left (fx next, echo, robot, again), long press = record by hand / stop.`}],steps:[`Wire the INMP441 (VDD 3V3, GND, L/R → GND, SCK 40, WS 41, SD 42) and the MAX98357A + speaker as in S7. Point the speaker away from the mic.`,`Flash <code>sound-lab-s8</code>, <code>vol 25</code>. <b>Checkpoint 1:</b> <code>Microphone: INMP441</code> and the parrot says “Say something!”.`,`Say a short sentence. <b>Checkpoint 2:</b> the chipmunk answers.`,`<code>fx giant</code>, <code>again</code>; <code>fx reverse</code>, <code>again</code>. <b>Checkpoint 3:</b> you can say why the giant is slow.`,`<code>echo on</code>, clap. <b>Checkpoint 4:</b> fading repeats; <code>echo off</code>.`,`<code>robot on</code>, talk. <b>Checkpoint 5:</b> a robot voice; <code>robot off</code>.`],note:`Changing the speed of a recording changes its pitch and its length together: that's why the chipmunk talks fast. Keeping the speed while shifting the pitch needs much more DSP (grains or a phase vocoder). Feedback: if what the mic hears comes back louder than it went out (loop gain above 1), it goes round and round: the howl. The guard cuts the live effect when the level keeps climbing (9 dB within 300 ms) or stays above −12 dBFS. Latency = one screen frame of processing + the speaker's queue + its 16 ms DMA block.`,preview:{widget:`sound`}}];export{e as soundLessons};
